# Squid dstdomain allowlist -- RUNTIME phase.
#
# The agents are allowed the public web. An agent confined to its training
# data cannot answer a question about today, which is most of the point of
# running one.
#
# What they are denied is the private network around them -- the host, its
# other services, the LAN, link-local. That denial is an ACL on destination
# ADDRESS in squid.conf, not a line in this file, and it is what stops an
# agent damaging the infrastructure it runs on.
#
# So this file is no longer the boundary. It names destinations that need a
# non-standard port, plus anything worth permitting explicitly. Everything
# on 80/443 is already reachable.
#
# Activate with:  sudo agent-egress runtime
# A leading dot matches subdomains. One entry per line.

# --- Models -----------------------------------------------------------------
apps.inside.anl.gov

# --- Local fallback model ---------------------------------------------------
# DGX Spark vLLM over Tailscale, port 8000. UNVERIFIED -- docs/failover.md.
spark-ts

# --- Chat channels ----------------------------------------------------------
# Who may MESSAGE a bot is the control that matters, and it is not this file:
# it is dmPolicy/allowFrom in OpenClaw and TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS in Hermes.
# verify.sh fails the build on a live channel with an empty allowlist.
api.telegram.org
